{:edb/db-before-t 1066 :edb/db-after-t 1067 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534379 50 #inst "2026-10-06T18:38:46.510Z" 13194139534379 true] [17592186175825 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284972 13194139534379 true] [17592186175825 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284986 13194139534379 true] [17592186175827 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284958 13194139534379 true] [17592186175827 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284965 13194139534379 true] [17592186175827 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284979 13194139534379 true] [17592186284958 1017 #edb/ref 17592186284959 13194139534379 true] [17592186284958 1089 "q-129177" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284958 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534379 true] [17592186284958 1100 "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{x-3}?$ \n\n- $[1,5]$\n- $[-5,-1]$\n- $(-5,3)$" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284958 1113 #uuid "43d2586d-6461-59a4-bb6c-891bfb0c3e55" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284958 1145 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[1,5]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on $[1,5]$ because this interval contains points where the function is not defined. In particular, it is not defined at $x=3.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-5,-1]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[-5,-1].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $(-5,3)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139534379 true] [17592186284959 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284960 13194139534379 true] [17592186284959 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284961 13194139534379 true] [17592186284959 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284962 13194139534379 true] [17592186284959 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284963 13194139534379 true] [17592186284959 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284964 13194139534379 true] [17592186284959 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534379 true] [17592186284959 1041 "selection" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284959 1104 #edb/ref 17592186284964 13194139534379 true] [17592186284959 1110 #uuid "c0fabbf6-49b2-54f9-bcb5-4502d72b1151" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284960 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284960 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534379 true] [17592186284960 1111 #uuid "85ecac57-8075-55ae-aae2-30e9278cba43" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284961 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284961 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534379 true] [17592186284961 1111 #uuid "ea3fdd5c-5823-5422-9723-af96cf44bb04" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284962 1034 "I only" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284962 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534379 true] [17592186284962 1111 #uuid "2be60a16-2143-586a-9ca8-1b5eff6fccf0" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284963 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284963 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534379 true] [17592186284963 1111 #uuid "03096323-9e9d-5339-b08c-10c02cf4fd59" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284964 1034 "II only" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284964 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534379 true] [17592186284964 1111 #uuid "48b7b091-1865-5ef2-a7fd-4bdbf37f968e" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284965 1017 #edb/ref 17592186284966 13194139534379 true] [17592186284965 1089 "q-129218" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284965 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534379 true] [17592186284965 1100 "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)}{x-2}?$ \n\n- $[-3,-1]$\n- $[-1,1]$\n- $[1,3]$" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284965 1113 #uuid "006674eb-77e7-5065-abd5-689be0ee86b6" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284965 1145 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-3,-1]$ is closed, but the function is not defined on the part of the interval where $x\\le -2.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-1,1]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[-1,1].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $[1,3]$ is closed, but the function is not defined at $x=2.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139534379 true] [17592186284966 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284967 13194139534379 true] [17592186284966 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284968 13194139534379 true] [17592186284966 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284969 13194139534379 true] [17592186284966 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284970 13194139534379 true] [17592186284966 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284971 13194139534379 true] [17592186284966 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534379 true] [17592186284966 1041 "selection" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284966 1104 #edb/ref 17592186284971 13194139534379 true] [17592186284966 1110 #uuid "9dc2f7b8-8dc0-5f60-bef8-3bd53d6d43c4" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284967 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284967 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534379 true] [17592186284967 1111 #uuid "9020e4d9-b374-5098-8464-1fa9148c0218" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284968 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284968 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534379 true] [17592186284968 1111 #uuid "faf47ff3-8a84-5dac-ad9b-ea5414bcdd8b" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284969 1034 "I only" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284969 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534379 true] [17592186284969 1111 #uuid "92e45437-1368-525c-851f-55c7bf9dc9e0" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284970 1034 "III only" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284970 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534379 true] [17592186284970 1111 #uuid "b7952500-070e-5a50-a1fc-c951e620e387" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284971 1034 "II only" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284971 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534379 true] [17592186284971 1111 #uuid "a08c8612-b665-5b8f-85ff-03547a601944" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284972 1017 #edb/ref 17592186284973 13194139534379 true] [17592186284972 1089 "q-131069" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284972 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534379 true] [17592186284972 1100 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{8}^{x}-61, & x<2 \\\\ {3}^{x-1}, & x\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[3,5]$\n- $[1,3]$\n- $(-1,1)$" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284972 1113 #uuid "5f87b34e-900b-509f-a06d-8ce3d9e460f1" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284972 1145 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[3,5].$ In this interval, $f(x)={3}^{x-1},$ so the function is continuous on the interval $[3,5].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider $[1,3].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}({8}^{x}-61)={8}^{2}-61=3, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}{3}^{x-1}={3}^{2-1}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=2$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[1,3],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, the interval $(-1,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139534379 true] [17592186284973 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284974 13194139534379 true] [17592186284973 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284975 13194139534379 true] [17592186284973 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284976 13194139534379 true] [17592186284973 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284977 13194139534379 true] [17592186284973 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284978 13194139534379 true] [17592186284973 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534379 true] [17592186284973 1041 "selection" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284973 1104 #edb/ref 17592186284977 13194139534379 true] [17592186284973 1110 #uuid "f263bd4c-3071-5a41-aec7-94b9e8e5a51e" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284974 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284974 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534379 true] [17592186284974 1111 #uuid "dc1020a2-491a-5b9c-9625-ba5f3d57e9ed" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284975 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284975 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534379 true] [17592186284975 1111 #uuid "c28ddfa2-c39b-58a9-a94c-272df8b08855" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284976 1034 "I only" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284976 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534379 true] [17592186284976 1111 #uuid "e13645c7-49b0-573c-9230-390949ff5fa5" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284977 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284977 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534379 true] [17592186284977 1111 #uuid "5f37b9f6-8c7b-5a2e-a9ff-182c9dd85487" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284978 1034 "II only" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284978 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534379 true] [17592186284978 1111 #uuid "e4434666-1415-5db1-b378-b5f799752005" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284979 1017 #edb/ref 17592186284980 13194139534379 true] [17592186284979 1089 "q-48020" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284979 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534379 true] [17592186284979 1100 "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)^{2}?$ \n\n- $[-1,0]$\n- $(-2,1]$\n- $[-3,2]$" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284979 1113 #uuid "e9e82046-8cdd-553b-baaa-4c24b4f6ef7b" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284979 1145 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-1,0]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[-1,0]$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $(-2,1]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-3,1]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)^{2}$ is not defined at $x=-2.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139534379 true] [17592186284980 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284981 13194139534379 true] [17592186284980 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284982 13194139534379 true] [17592186284980 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284983 13194139534379 true] [17592186284980 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284984 13194139534379 true] [17592186284980 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284985 13194139534379 true] [17592186284980 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534379 true] [17592186284980 1041 "selection" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284980 1104 #edb/ref 17592186284982 13194139534379 true] [17592186284980 1110 #uuid "b939ff1b-4b80-5bd7-baaf-2036a5177405" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284981 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284981 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534379 true] [17592186284981 1111 #uuid "cea1e6b7-3be5-5316-a034-b1cbe3ec4e62" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284982 1034 "I only" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284982 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534379 true] [17592186284982 1111 #uuid "2932f71c-ec81-5cd4-8cba-7aa6bcf25d37" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284983 1034 "III only" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284983 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534379 true] [17592186284983 1111 #uuid "8b3038fd-7515-576d-a82c-0d488a6b1407" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284984 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284984 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534379 true] [17592186284984 1111 #uuid "8ba94e8e-b357-5f17-b5d9-285b27a926ff" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284985 1034 "II only" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284985 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534379 true] [17592186284985 1111 #uuid "66c7fbff-f46d-5a46-bb7e-6b45fce7973a" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284986 1017 #edb/ref 17592186284987 13194139534379 true] [17592186284986 1089 "q-73531" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284986 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534379 true] [17592186284986 1100 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{x-1}-1}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ \\,\\,1, & x=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[-2,2]$\n- $[-3,1)$\n- $[1.5,2]$" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284986 1113 #uuid "85230c8e-20d5-5891-94a2-6cb0ace2528a" 13194139534379 true] [17592186284986 1145 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- Let first consider the interval  $[-2,2].$ This is a  closed interval, but we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{2}^{x-1}-1}=\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1]$, and the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-3,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\n- Finally consider  $[1.5,2].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=1,$ which is not in the interval  $[1.5,2].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[1.5,2]$, and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139534379 true] [17592186284987 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284988 13194139534379 true] [17592186284987 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284989 13194139534379 true] [17592186284987 1000 #edb/ref 1759